/* CSFLOAT-1:1 PARITY ship #5800 — sticky <thead> on /db.
   csfloat /db has a 118px tall .header-container sticky at top:0 z:999.
   The local /db table is INSIDE a modal/page surface that scrolls; sticky
   thead inside the table needs position:sticky with top:0 (the surface
   scrolls, not window) and a z above row content. Cells must carry the
   sticky bg — Safari/Chrome both ignore sticky on <thead> alone for tables
   with border-collapse:collapse, so per-th sticky is the only reliable
   path. Border-bottom stays the existing 1px var(--line); a subtle drop
   shadow appears under the row to signal "header floats above content"
   the moment any row scrolls behind it. */
body .db-table thead th {
  position: sticky !important;
  top: 0 !important;
  z-index: 5 !important;
  background: var(--bg-2) !important;
  /* shadow only renders when something passes under — paint-cheap. */
  box-shadow: 0 1px 0 0 var(--line), 0 4px 8px -4px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35) !important;
  /* sticky cells must paint before tbody — clip overflow so radius works
     if any future variant adds rounded corners to the header. */
  background-clip: padding-box !important;
}
/* When the table sits in a modal that scrolls, the thead's sticky context
   is the closest scrolling ancestor — usually .db-table-scroll. Force it
   to be the offset parent by making it position:relative without changing
   layout. Already relative in most builds; no-op safety net. */
body .db-table-scroll { position: relative !important; }
/* END CSFLOAT-1:1 PARITY ship #5800 */

/* CSFLOAT-1:1 PARITY ship #5801 — .db-pager table footer polish.
   Existing .db-pager: padding 14px 16px, border-top 1px var(--line),
   bg var(--bg). csfloat keeps the page surface seamless (no separate bg
   stripe between table and pager); the pager floats on the same surface
   with a hairline divider above. Bring the pager bg to transparent so the
   parent surface shows through, tighten the vertical padding to 12px to
   match csfloat's 12px button-row rhythm, and pin a min-height of 56px so
   the buttons never crowd the indicator. */
body .db-pager {
  background: transparent !important;
  padding: 12px 16px !important;
  min-height: 56px !important;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--line) !important;
  /* csfloat aligns prev/indicator/next on a single baseline; flex gap of
     12px keeps the indicator from kissing the buttons on narrow widths. */
  gap: 12px !important;
}
body .db-pager .btn-ghost {
  /* csfloat pager buttons are pill-tight: ~32px tall, 12px horizontal,
     subtle border, no fill. Match exactly. */
  height: 32px !important;
  padding: 0 12px !important;
  font-size: 12px !important;
  border: 1px solid var(--border) !important;
  border-radius: 8px !important;
  background: transparent !important;
  color: var(--ink-2) !important;
  transition: border-color 140ms ease, color 140ms ease, background 140ms ease !important;
}
body .db-pager .btn-ghost:not(:disabled):hover {
  border-color: var(--ink-3) !important;
  color: var(--ink) !important;
  background: var(--bg-2) !important;
}
body .db-pager .btn-ghost:disabled {
  opacity: 0.45 !important;
  cursor: default !important;
}
body .db-pager > span {
  /* page indicator chip styling — mono, tabular nums, ink-3. */
  font-family: var(--mono) !important;
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums !important;
  font-size: 12px !important;
  color: var(--ink-3) !important;
  letter-spacing: 0.02em !important;
}
/* END CSFLOAT-1:1 PARITY ship #5801 */

/* CSFLOAT-1:1 PARITY ship #5802 — .db-meta caption strip.
   This is the "X results · page Y / Z" strip ABOVE the table — the
   functional analogue of csfloat's app-skin-counter (text "1,871,925,394
   Skins", fs:18px, color:white, h:22, bg transparent). Local existing
   .db-meta: padding 6px 0 14px, fs 12px, color ink-4. csfloat's counter
   is bigger and brighter — bump fs to 13px (we have a richer label
   "results · page X / Y" so 18px would dominate), keep bg transparent,
   pad 10px 0 14px so the strip floats clearly between the controls and
   the table. The numeric <strong> tags get csfloat's white-on-dark
   accent treatment. */
body .db-meta {
  padding: 10px 0 14px !important;
  font-size: 13px !important;
  color: var(--ink-3) !important;
  background: transparent !important;
  /* tabular nums so "300 results" and "3,000 results" align across loads */
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums !important;
  letter-spacing: 0.005em !important;
  display: flex !important;
  align-items: baseline !important;
  gap: 6px !important;
  flex-wrap: wrap !important;
}
body .db-meta strong {
  color: var(--ink) !important;
  font-family: var(--mono) !important;
  font-weight: 600 !important;
  font-size: 13px !important;
  letter-spacing: 0 !important;
}
/* END CSFLOAT-1:1 PARITY ship #5802 */

/* CSFLOAT-1:1 PARITY ship #5803 — density toggle (compact ↔ comfortable).
   csfloat /db itself does NOT ship a density toggle (verified — no
   compact/comfortable/density-labelled buttons in app-float-dbtable on
   csfloat.com /db). Local /db likewise does not currently render one in
   markup. This ship therefore adds the CSS HOOK so that if the JS layer
   later attaches `data-density="compact"` to the .db-table-scroll
   wrapper (or the table itself), the row height + cell padding compress
   without any further restyle. Comfortable (default) keeps the existing
   64px row height + 14px cell padding (Boss QA D4); compact drops to a
   csfloat-style 44px row + 8px 12px padding. Strictly opt-in via the
   data attribute so no current pixel changes. */
body .db-table-scroll[data-density="compact"] .db-table tbody tr,
body .db-table[data-density="compact"] tbody tr {
  height: 44px !important;
}
body .db-table-scroll[data-density="compact"] .db-table tbody td,
body .db-table[data-density="compact"] tbody td {
  padding: 8px 12px !important;
  font-size: 12.5px !important;
}
body .db-table-scroll[data-density="compact"] .db-table thead th,
body .db-table[data-density="compact"] thead th {
  padding: 8px 12px !important;
}
body .db-table-scroll[data-density="compact"] .db-table .db-thumb,
body .db-table[data-density="compact"] .db-thumb {
  width: 32px !important;
  height: 32px !important;
  min-width: 32px !important;
  border-radius: 6px !important;
  font-size: 13px !important;
}
/* Comfortable explicit override (default values, kept for symmetry so a
   JS toggle can flip back without leaving the row in a half-state). */
body .db-table-scroll[data-density="comfortable"] .db-table tbody tr,
body .db-table[data-density="comfortable"] tbody tr {
  height: 64px !important;
}
body .db-table-scroll[data-density="comfortable"] .db-table tbody td,
body .db-table[data-density="comfortable"] tbody td {
  padding: 14px 14px !important;
  font-size: 13px !important;
}
/* END CSFLOAT-1:1 PARITY ship #5803 */

/* CSFLOAT-1:1 PARITY ship #5804 — selected row highlight.
   csfloat /db rows are clickable and use a subtle accent-tint on hover
   (already shipped via earlier .db-row:hover). For a selected/active row
   (e.g. keyboard-focused, or when the modal carries an "open detail"
   state with the row visually anchored) we want a stronger but still
   subtle treatment: 8% accent-mix bg (versus 4% on hover), a 2px-wide
   left accent rail on the first cell, and ink color bumped to var(--ink)
   for the row's text content. The trigger is `.db-row.is-selected`,
   `.db-row[aria-selected="true"]`, or `.db-row.is-active` so any of the
   common React/A11y patterns latch on without extra wiring. Focus-visible
   on a row's <a.db-name> child also opts the row in via :has() so
   keyboard users see the same anchor as mouse-clickers. */
body .db-table tbody tr.db-row.is-selected,
body .db-table tbody tr.db-row[aria-selected="true"],
body .db-table tbody tr.db-row.is-active,
body .db-table tbody tr.db-row:has(a.db-name:focus-visible) {
  background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--cta) 8%, transparent) !important;
}
body .db-table tbody tr.db-row.is-selected td,
body .db-table tbody tr.db-row[aria-selected="true"] td,
body .db-table tbody tr.db-row.is-active td,
body .db-table tbody tr.db-row:has(a.db-name:focus-visible) td {
  color: var(--ink) !important;
}
body .db-table tbody tr.db-row.is-selected td:first-child,
body .db-table tbody tr.db-row[aria-selected="true"] td:first-child,
body .db-table tbody tr.db-row.is-active td:first-child,
body .db-table tbody tr.db-row:has(a.db-name:focus-visible) td:first-child {
  /* 2px accent rail on the first cell — csfloat's grid-row selection
     pattern (used in the inventory selector). Inset box-shadow keeps the
     rail from forcing an extra px of width. */
  box-shadow: inset 2px 0 0 0 var(--cta) !important;
}
/* The row's <a.db-name> shouldn't double-paint a focus ring inside an
   already highlighted row — drop the outline only when the row's parent
   carries the selection state, otherwise leave default a11y outline. */
body .db-table tbody tr.db-row.is-selected a.db-name:focus-visible,
body .db-table tbody tr.db-row[aria-selected="true"] a.db-name:focus-visible,
body .db-table tbody tr.db-row.is-active a.db-name:focus-visible {
  outline: none !important;
}
/* END CSFLOAT-1:1 PARITY ship #5804 */

